# -*- mode: org; -*-

* 0.0.5-pre

** New features and UI changes

- ~DiscussionControl cleanup~ removes a finished discussion's generated
  worktree, keeping its transcript and branch. Optional ~delete_branch: true~
  also attempts safe Git branch deletion. Cleanup refuses unfinished work and
  never forces Git removal; status reports retain metadata and cleanup state,
  including when branch deletion must be retried after integration.

- ~Discussion~ accepts ~worktree: true~ to create a randomly named Git
  worktree and branch from the selected ~directory~, or the caller's directory.
  The child starts at the new worktree root. Creation and status results
  include the path, branch, source repository/directory and starting commit.
  Worktrees start at the source's committed HEAD and remain after closing.

- The bundled ~executor~ agent has been removed. The default agent now
  handles implementation and verification directly, with ~researcher~ and
  ~result-explorer~ available for delegated investigation.

- File review now offers ~A~ to ask about a proposal in an independent gptel
  aside, from the queue or either Ediff pane. It inherits conversation context
  and settings, includes the displayed revision and selected lines, and keeps
  questions and answers out of the original chat. Asking leaves the review
  decision unchanged. Follow-ups use ~C-c RET~; ~C-c C-r~ returns to Ediff.

- All gptel conversations now discover scoped ~AGENTS.md~ instructions, with private
  per-conversation registries and replaceable per-call instruction blocks.
  File mutations defer when their scope or instruction versions require a new
  model decision. ~gptel-agent-toggle-project-instructions~ controls the local
  preference; ~gptel-agent-describe-project-instructions~ shows discovered and
  delivered instructions. ~gptel-agent-use-project-instructions~ controls the
  global default regardless of preset, with per-buffer overrides.

- ~AskUserQuestion~ now queues questions with ~action: "add"~ (the default)
  and returns immediately. Repeated calls extend one live question buffer,
  preserving saved answers. ~action: "wait"~ explicitly pauses the turn and
  collects all answers or cancellation. Users can save answers while the agent
  works and submit when it waits. ~gptel-agent-questions~ reopens the queue.
  If the model tries to finish with an uncollected batch, the runtime keeps the
  turn open and waits automatically, then resumes with the submitted answers
  or cancellation. Pending questions are no longer discarded on early completion.
  Question windows now open below their owning conversation, leaving unrelated
  windows alone. ~gptel-agent-questions-display~ can instead use ~same-window~
  to temporarily replace that conversation. Hidden conversations keep their
  questions queued until explicitly opened.

- ~Discussion~ creates independent, editable gptel conversations that survive
  their parent's turn. ~DiscussionControl~ monitors, steers, follows up, waits,
  answers questions and closes discussions. Tool approvals stay in the child
  buffer for the user to approve or reject; an echo-area notice identifies it.
  Replies and attention alerts stay in the child. The child uses
  ~DiscussionReport~ to send a chosen answer or useful update; only explicit
  reports wake idle parents or arrive at safe request boundaries. Parents can
  request reports with ~send~ or inspect work with ~read~. Unsent drafts defer
  automatic sending. ~gptel-agent-discussions~
  opens the conversation list; users can interact directly in each child chat.

- ~Agent~ accepts ~background: true~ for immediate launch acknowledgements.
  ~AgentControl~ lists, inspects, waits for, steers and cancels owned workers.
  Completion reports arrive at safe request boundaries. A request-local gate
  keeps parent turns open until all descendants finish and their outcomes are
  delivered, including when a model attempts an early final answer. Blocking
  calls and retained-conversation follow-ups remain supported.

- Oversized ~Bash~ output is now saved in full and returned as a bounded
  preview with an exact extraction query.  A new ~result-explorer~ agent
  can analyze only that registered result file through harness-scoped
  read/search tools and returns line-cited evidence for parent verification.

- Sub-agent time and model-round budgets are now configurable per agent with
  the ~task-timeout~ and ~max-request-rounds~ Markdown frontmatter or Org
  property keys.  Omitted limits inherit the corresponding global fallback
  options, which now default to nil.  The built-in researcher allows 3600
  seconds and 100 rounds, and result-explorer allows 1800 seconds and 60 rounds.
  The default web-tool budget is 50 calls per sub-agent run.

- gptel-agent now works over TRAMP.  This means it can run processes on
  remote servers, grep and edit files and so on without the need to
  install Emacs or an agent harness.  This involves calling command line
  programs on the remote, so it requires a POSIX-y remote environment.

- New user option ~gptel-agent-preset~ to specify additional gptel
  settings for sub-agents.  This is a preset that applies to all
  sub-agent calls, before any agent-specific settings.

  Its main purpose is to allow using a cheaper/smaller (or larger!)
  model for sub-agent tasks, which is a common need depending on the
  estimated difficulty of such tasks.  Of course, this is a gptel preset
  so you are not restricted to specifying a backend/model -- any gptel
  setting is supported.

  It can be set to a preset name (a symbol) or a plist spec, for example:
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (gptel-make-preset 'lite              ;elsewhere
    :backend "Gemini"
    :model 'gemini-flash-lite-latest)

  ;; Apply the "lite" preset to sub-agent calls
  (setq gptel-agent-preset 'lite)
  #+end_src
  or just
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (setq gptel-agent-preset
        '(:backend "Gemini" :model gemini-flash-lite-latest))
  #+end_src

- New command ~gptel-agent-compact~ to compact/summarize a conversation
  and free up the context window.  It works on the buffer up to the
  cursor position or on the active region, so you can compact a part of
  a conversation if desired.  Currently this must be invoked manually,
  but auto-compaction is planned for the future.

  Be warned: this is lossy and modifies the chat buffer!

  The prompt used for compaction is customizable, see
  ~gptel-agent-compact-prompt~.  You can also specify additional
  compaction instructions interactively by calling ~gptel-agent-compact~
  with a prefix argument.

  This command is experimental, and the implementation of prompt
  compaction might change in the future.

** Notable bug fixes

- ~Process~ returns invalid read/wait arguments and other operation errors to
  the model instead of aborting the request with a misleading HTTP 200 error.
  Tail-view guidance now explicitly says to omit ~offset~, including zero.

- Loading a skill preserves globally configured conversation tools across
  turns. Discussions inherit the parent's full active tool list, including
  tools activated by skills, without inheriting skill pre-approvals. An explicit
  preset can override the child's tools; ~DiscussionReport~ remains available.

- Discussion approval cleanup now supports gptel versions without the private
  overlay cleanup helper, preventing failed approvals from stranding worker
  tools. Worker ~TodoWrite~ calls use their own request and diagnostic buffer;
  completing a chat's todo list also clears its active-task header immediately.

- ~WebFetch~ now handles textual responses according to their content
  type instead of sending JSON, source files and plain text through the
  HTML readability parser.  HTML extraction falls back to a full-page
  render or the decoded response body, HTTP failures include status and
  response excerpts, and oversized output is bounded with an explicit
  truncation notice.  Requests remain noninteractive, and YouTube URLs
  are routed through the same tool to return descriptions and transcripts.

- Restored ~gptel-agent-preset~ application for sub-agent requests.  The
  option is read from the owning parent buffer and applied independently
  before per-agent settings, preserving named-preset parents,
  composition forms and pre/post hooks.  Unknown named presets now
  report an error instead of being silently ignored.

- Sub-agents now receive an explicit completion contract: negative and
  inconclusive findings are valid outcomes and must include attempts,
  limitations and confidence.  A repeated/cyclic call is blocked once
  to allow a strategy change; continued looping or an exhausted web
  budget now closes exploration and gives the same sub-agent a tool-free
  finalization turn.  The sub-agent retains its full transcript and must
  interpret the evidence and return its own final answer; raw tool output
  is not substituted for an agent response.

- The built-in researcher now prefers the current workspace for codebase
  tasks, treats sufficient successful results as a stop signal, and uses
  GitHub repository metadata instead of guessing branch names.

- ~WebSearch~ now defaults to 10 results, supports a configurable bound
  with ~gptel-agent-web-search-default-count~ and
  ~gptel-agent-web-search-max-count~, and returns query/count/coverage
  metadata.  Its instructions discourage identical retries and direct
  agents to fetch URLs derived from structured identifiers directly.

